<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sobhuza I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1780–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Somhlolo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sobhuza I, also known as Somhlolo, was king of the Swazi from about 1815 to 1836. Driven north from the Pongola river by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>, he settled his people in the heart of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>, absorbed local clans and avoided war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> through diplomacy and marriage alliances. He is regarded as a founder of the Swazi nation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sobhuza, also known as Ngwane IV, was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndvungunye">Ndvungunye</a>, also called Zikodze, who died after being struck by lightning, and of Somnjalose Simelane. His name Somhlolo means man of mysteries. Somnjalose was the younger sister and inhlanti, or support bride, of Ndvungunye's senior wife Lojiba Simelane, who had no sons. Under the Ngwane royal rules of kinship Sobhuza counted as Lojiba's son, so when he became king it was Lojiba, not his own mother, who became Indlovukati, the Queen Mother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The kingdom he inherited, KaNgwane, was centred along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pongola-river">Pongola River</a> south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>. The Dlamini kings, who had earlier ruled in and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubombo-mountains">Lubombo Mountains</a>, had built it under his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwane-iii">Ngwane III</a> around 1750 by taking in more than a dozen smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. In the 1810s and 1820s it was threatened by the stronger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kingdoms, and Sobhuza moved his royal centre from Zombodze in the Shiselweni region north to a new Zombodze in central Eswatini, leaving the old centres as southern outposts.</p><p>From there he conquered many local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, and KaNgwane became a kingdom comparable to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, the Pedi and the Maroti. To make peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a>, with whom his people were constantly in conflict, he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thandile">Tsandzile Ndwandwe</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>. She bore him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a> and Mzamose Dlamini. The clans conquered before and during his rule are known as the Emakhandzambili.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sobhuza was buried at the royal burial site of Mbilaneni. After a short regency by Lojiba, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a> succeeded him, with Tsandzile as Queen Mother. He left a country said to reach Barberton in the north, Carolina in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pongola-river">Pongola River</a> in the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubombo-mountains">Lubombo Mountains</a> in the east, larger than present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>. He is greatly revered in Eswatini, where Swazis mark Somhlolo Day on 6 September as their Independence Day and the national stadium bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sobhuza I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive change came under <b>Sobhuza I</b>, also called Somhlolo (r. c. 1815–c. 1839).</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zwide organised regiments and dominated his neighbours, driving <b>Sobhuza I's</b> people north and killing Dingiswayo of the Mthethwa Paramountcy around 1818.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.48,31.43&amp;year=1816" aria-label="Eswatini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sobhuza I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5110 4700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5110 4700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.48,31.43&amp;year=1816">What was here in 1816? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
